.TH UNABASHED 1 2026-11-09 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
unabashed, sh, ash, lash, bash \- a bash 5.3 shell for Windows
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sh
.RI [ options ]
.B \-c
.I command
.RI [ name
.RI [ argument ...]]
.br
.B sh
.RI [ options ]
.I file
.RI [ argument ...]
.br
.B sh
.RI [ options ]
.RB [ \-s ]
.RI [ argument ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
unabashed is a shell written to behave as GNU bash 5.3 does, for Windows.
It runs commands read from a string, a file, or standard input.
In w64devkit the one program is installed four times, as
.BR sh ,
.BR ash ,
.BR lash ,
and
.BR bash ,
and the name it is started under chooses how it treats paths, the
environment, and the programs it runs.
The synopsis shows
.BR sh ,
but every name accepts the same forms.
.PP
This page describes what is particular to unabashed: its two modes,
invocation, startup, paths, devices, how it finds and runs Windows
programs, and where it differs from bash.
It does not teach the language, which is bash's.
GNU publishes the Bash Reference Manual at
.IR https://www.gnu.org/software/bash/manual/ ,
and the
.B help
builtin describes each builtin command:
.B help
alone lists them,
.B help \-d
.I name
gives a one-line summary, and
.B help \-m
.I name
prints one in the form of a manual page.
.SS Names and modes
The mode comes from the name the shell is started under, which is the
first word of its command line as Windows splits it, not the file name
of the executable.
The file name stands in only when the command line has no words, and
an empty first word gives msys mode.
The shell removes one leading
.BR \- ,
then everything up to the last
.BR / ,
.BR \e ,
or
.BR : ,
then one trailing
.B .exe
in any case.
If what remains is
.BR sh ,
.BR ash ,
or
.BR lash ,
in any case, the shell is in
.IR "native mode" :
Windows paths such as
.IR C:/Users/me ,
and the conventions of busybox-w32's shell, which the kit used before.
Any other name, such as
.BR bash ,
.BR rbash ,
or
.BR unabashed ,
gives
.IR "msys mode" :
POSIX paths such as
.I /c/Users/me
over a mount table, as MSYS2's and Git for Windows' bash have them.
So
.BR SH.EXE ,
.BR \-lash ,
and
.I C:\ew64devkit\ebin\esh.exe
are native, and a parent that runs
.I bash.exe
with a command line whose first word is
.B sh
gets native mode.
.PP
An
.I etc/fstab
in the install root selects msys mode for every name, including
.BR sh ,
as in an MSYS2 or Git for Windows installation, where
.B sh
is bash.
The install root is the directory of the executable, minus a final
.I /usr/bin
or
.IR /bin ,
matched in any case; when the directory ends in neither, it is the
install root itself.
Only whether the file exists decides the mode; its contents are the
mount table (see Paths in msys mode below).
w64devkit ships no
.IR etc/fstab .
.PP
Started as
.BR cygpath ,
an msys name, the program does not run as a shell: it acts as the
.B cygpath
program on its arguments and exits.
.B cygpath \-\-help
lists its options;
.B help
has no topic for it.
.PP
The hidden
.B shopt
option
.B msyspath
switches mode at run time:
.B shopt \-s msyspath
selects msys mode and
.B shopt \-u msyspath
native mode.
It exists only in the Windows build, and neither
.B shopt
nor
.B shopt \-p
lists it, nor does
.BR BASHOPTS ,
but
.B shopt
.BR msyspath ,
.BR "shopt \-q" ,
and the
.B \-O
and
.B +O
options accept it.
Switching rewrites
.B PATH
and
.B PWD
in the new form and forgets the remembered command locations.
It also changes what a leading
.B /
means, the
.B PATH
separator, the form in which paths print, which carriage returns the
parser drops, and whether
.BR "set \-o igncr" ,
.BR "shopt completion_strip_exe" ,
and the
.B cygpath
builtin exist.
It also changes how the shell finds and runs programs: the extensions
it tries, which files count as executable, whether it skips busybox
stubs, how it runs a script without
.B #!\&
and looks up a
.B #!\&
interpreter, and whether it converts the arguments and environment it
passes to a Windows program (see Running programs below), as well as
whether it reads
.B TZ
zone files.
It does not import the environment again or change the startup rules,
which follow the name the shell started under, and
.BR BASH ,
.BR OSTYPE ,
.BR MACHTYPE ,
.BR HOSTTYPE ,
.BR HOME ,
.BR SHELL ,
and
.B TERM
keep their values.
The shell itself passes
.B \-O msyspath
or
.B +O msyspath
to the copy of itself that runs a script meant for this shell, such as
a
.B #!/bin/sh
or
.B #!/bin/bash
script, so the script runs in the parent's mode (see Running programs
below).
Only the mode carries over.
The copy is started under the executable's own path, so its identity
variables, environment import, and login and startup rules follow the
file name, not the parent's name: a script run by
.I bash.exe
started as
.B sh
sees
.B OSTYPE
.BR cygwin ,
and one run by
.B lash
is a login shell and reads the login files again.
.PP
The variables that identify the shell follow the mode it started in.
In native mode
.B OSTYPE
is
.BR win32 ,
.B MACHTYPE
is
.B x86_64\-w64\-mingw32
.RB ( i686\-w64\-mingw32
in the 32-bit build), and
.B BASH
is the executable's full Windows path with forward slashes, such as
.IR C:/w64devkit/bin/sh.exe .
In msys mode
.B OSTYPE
is
.BR cygwin ,
.B MACHTYPE
is
.B x86_64\-pc\-cygwin
.RB ( i686\-pc\-cygwin ),
and
.B BASH
is the executable's POSIX path without a trailing
.BR .exe ,
such as
.IR /bin/bash .
In both modes
.B HOSTTYPE
is
.B x86_64
or
.BR i686 ,
.B BASH_VERSION
is
.BR 5.3.0(1)\-release ,
and
.B BASH_VERSINFO
is
.BR "(5 3 0 1 release" " \fIMACHTYPE\fB)\fR."
.PP
The name also bears on whether the shell is a login shell: a name
beginning with
.B \-
makes one in both modes, and in native mode so does
.B lash
(see Invocation below).
A name of
.BR rbash ,
after the same trimming, makes a restricted shell in msys mode.
.SS Invocation
In native mode the shell's arguments are exactly as Windows splits its
command line (the rules of
.BR CommandLineToArgvW ),
and
.B $0
keeps the first word as given, backslashes and
.B .exe
included.
.PP
In msys mode the shell splits its command line again, as the MSYS2
runtime does for a program started by a Windows parent:
.IP \(bu 2
Double and single quotes both group words, by Cygwin's rules.
Inside quotes two backslashes stand for one, where a Windows program
would keep both, so
.B \(dqC:\e\ex\(dq
gives
.IR C:\ex ,
and inside double quotes
.B \e\(dq
and
.B \(dq\(dq
stand for
.BR \(dq .
.IP \(bu 2
An unquoted
.BI @ file
naming a readable regular file is replaced by the words in that file,
which may name further response files, up to 10 in all.
An eleventh prints
.IB name ": @" file ": too many levels of response files"
and the shell exits with status 1.
An
.BI @ file
that names no file, or a directory, stays as it is.
.IP \(bu 2
An unquoted word containing
.BR * ,
.BR ? ,
or
.BR [ ,
or beginning with
.BR ~ ,
is expanded as a file name pattern, and stays as it is when nothing
matches, except that a leading
.B ~
alone or before
.B /
first becomes the value of
.B HOME
when that names a directory, so
.B ~/nosuch
stays as
.IR /c/Users/me/nosuch .
A word containing
.B {
first becomes one word for each alternative in its braces, whether or
not any file matches, so
.B q{1,2}
becomes
.B q1
and
.BR q2 ,
and each of those is then expanded as a pattern.
.IP \(bu 2
A first word containing
.B :
or
.B \e
is converted to a POSIX path through the mount table and loses a
trailing
.B .exe
in any case, so in a kit installed at
.IR C:\ew64devkit ,
.I C:\ew64devkit\ebin\ebash.exe
gives
.B $0
the value
.IR /bin/bash ,
and
.I C:\ex\ebash.exe
gives
.IR /c/x/bash .
A first word without either, such as
.BR bash.exe ,
is kept as it is.
.PP
A parent that is itself unabashed quotes the command line for whichever
of these the child will use.
.PP
With
.BR \-c ,
the shell runs
.IR command ,
the first argument after the options, so
.B sh \-c \-e
.I command
also works.
.I name
becomes
.BR $0 ,
by default the shell's own first word, and the remaining arguments
become the positional parameters.
.B BASH_EXECUTION_STRING
holds
.IR command .
.PP
Given a
.IR file ,
the shell runs the commands in it, with
.I file
as
.B $0
and the arguments after it as the positional parameters.
A
.I file
that does not exist and contains no
.BR / ,
.BR \e ,
or drive prefix is looked for along
.BR PATH ,
whose separator is
.B ;
in native mode.
.BR $0 ,
.BR BASH_SOURCE ,
and the start of each message keep the name given; only a syntax error
in the file names the file found.
A
.I file
that is not found gives status 127, and a directory, a binary file, or
any other read error gives status 126.
.PP
Otherwise, or with
.BR \-s ,
the shell reads commands from standard input,
.B $0
is its first word, and the arguments become the positional parameters.
.PP
A shell without
.B \-c
that reads standard input is interactive when standard input and
standard error are both consoles.
Only a Windows console counts: the pipe that mintty and other terminal
emulators provide does not, so a shell under one needs
.BR \-i ,
and
.B test \-t
is false there.
.B test \-t
is also false for
.I nul
and other character devices.
.B \-i
makes any shell interactive.
An interactive shell turns on job control when standard input or
standard error is a console.
In msys mode, an interactive shell with neither prints bash's two
warnings that it has no job control; native mode prints nothing.
.PP
Messages from a non-interactive shell begin with
.B $0
as it was given, so a native shell started by its full path prints
that path.
An interactive shell's messages begin with its name without the
directory and
.BR .exe .
.PP
A login shell is one started with
.B \-l
or
.BR \-\-login ,
or under a name that begins with
.BR \- ,
and in native mode also one whose name, after that trimming, begins
with
.B l
or
.BR L ,
which among the native names means
.BR lash .
The kit's launcher,
.BR w64devkit.exe ,
starts
.BR "sh \-l" .
In native mode a login shell reads its login files whether or not it is
interactive.
In msys mode a login shell in POSIX mode reads no login files.
Otherwise a shell named with
.B \-
reads them only when it is interactive, while
.B \-l
reads them in any shell.
The read-only
.B shopt
option
.B login_shell
reports the result.
No login shell changes directory.
.PP
A shell is restricted when started as
.BR rbash ,
or with
.B \-r
or
.BR \-\-restricted .
Such a shell ignores
.BR SHELLOPTS ,
.BR BASHOPTS ,
and functions in the environment, and the restrictions begin after the
startup files.
.PP
POSIX mode begins with
.BR \-\-posix ,
.BR "\-o posix" ,
or
.B posix
in an inherited
.BR SHELLOPTS ,
or when
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is in the environment, with any value, even empty.
An msys shell named
.BR sh ,
which needs an
.IR etc/fstab ,
enters POSIX mode after reading its startup files, as bash does.
A native shell never enters POSIX mode by its name, so in w64devkit
.B sh
is bash in its usual mode.
.SS Startup files
In native mode the startup files are busybox-w32's, whatever other
options say.
A login shell, unless given
.BR \-\-noprofile ,
reads these, in order, skipping any that is missing or unreadable:
.IP \(bu 2
.I /etc/profile
on the drive of the Windows directory
.RI ( C:/etc/profile
when that is unknown);
.IP \(bu 2
.I ../etc/profile
relative to the shell's own directory, which is the kit's
.IR etc/profile ;
.IP \(bu 2
.IR $HOME/.profile ,
when
.B HOME
is set, even to an empty value.
.PP
An interactive shell, login or not, then reads the file named by
.BR ENV ,
whose value is expanded but whose leading
.B ~
is not.
Native mode reads nothing else: not
.BR BASH_ENV ,
.IR ~/.bashrc ,
or logout files.
.BR \-\-norc ,
.BR \-\-rcfile ,
and
.B \-\-init\-file
have no effect there, and
.B \-\-posix
does not change the files read.
.PP
In msys mode the files are bash's, with paths through the mount table,
so that
.I /etc
is the install root's
.I etc
directory:
.IP \(bu 2
A login shell, as described under Invocation above, reads
.I /etc/profile
and then the first that exists of
.IR ~/.bash_profile ,
.IR ~/.bash_login ,
and
.IR ~/.profile .
.IP \(bu 2
A non-interactive shell reads the file named by
.BR BASH_ENV ,
after any login files.
.IP \(bu 2
An interactive shell that is not a login shell reads
.I /etc/bash.bashrc
and then
.IR ~/.bashrc ,
or in its place the file given by
.B \-\-rcfile
or
.BR \-\-init\-file .
.B \-\-norc
skips both.
.IP \(bu 2
In POSIX mode, a non-interactive shell reads nothing, and an
interactive one reads only the file named by
.BR ENV .
.IP \(bu 2
A shell named
.B sh
reads
.I /etc/profile
and
.I ~/.profile
as a login shell, never
.BR BASH_ENV ,
and when interactive the file named by
.B ENV
in place of the bashrc files, even with
.BR \-\-norc .
.PP
A leading
.B ~
in these names, and in the value of
.B ENV
or
.BR BASH_ENV ,
is expanded from
.BR HOME .
A missing file is skipped, and a directory or an unreadable file is
reported.
.PP
In msys mode a login shell that ends, either as an interactive shell or
by the
.B exit
builtin outside a subshell, reads
.I ~/.bash_logout
and then
.IR /etc/bash.bash_logout ,
once, keeping its exit status.
.B \-\-noprofile
does not stop them.
.PP
An interactive shell sets
.B HISTFILE
when it is unset, to
.I $HOME/.bash_history
in the form of
.B HOME
for the mode, or to
.I $HOME/.sh_history
when already in POSIX mode by the time options are parsed.
A shell that enters POSIX mode by its name does so later, and keeps
.IR .bash_history .
The history file is read after the startup files.
.SS "The two modes compared"
Each entry gives msys mode first and native mode second.
.TP
paths shown
In msys mode
.BR pwd ,
.BR PWD ,
and
.B \(ti
are POSIX paths such as
.IR /c/Users/me ,
except that an inherited
.B PWD
naming the working directory in Windows form is kept as it is, as is
the result of
.B cd
to a drive-relative name such as
.IR C:Users .
In native mode they are Windows paths with forward slashes and an
uppercase drive letter, such as
.IR C:/Users/me .
.TP
.RI "a leading " /
In msys mode
.I /
is the install root, and other directories come from a mount table (see
Paths in msys mode below).
In native mode
.I /x
is
.I x
at the root of the current drive or network share.
.TP
.RI "drive letters and " /c
In msys mode
.I /c/x
is
.I C:/x
(by default
.I /cygdrive/c/x
when there is an
.IR etc/fstab ),
and
.I C:/x
and
.I C:\ex
are accepted as input, with
.I C:
alone meaning the root of drive C.
In native mode
.I C:x
is relative to drive C's working directory, and
.I /c/x
is a directory named
.I c
on the current drive.
.TP
.BR PATH " separator"
In msys mode
.BR PATH ,
.BR CDPATH ,
and other lists are split on every
.BR : ,
so their elements must be in POSIX form.
In native mode they are split on
.BR ; ,
and assigning
.B PATH
turns its colons into semicolons, except after a drive letter.
.TP
environment import
In msys mode
.BR PATH ,
.BR HOME ,
.BR SHELL ,
.BR TMPDIR ,
.BR TMP ,
.BR TEMP ,
and a few others are converted to POSIX form at startup.
In native mode, when the environment holds a mixed-case
.BR Path ,
.BR ComSpec ,
or
.BR ProgramData ,
as one from a Windows program, Git Bash, or an msys mode shell does,
every variable name is made uppercase and backslashes in values other
than
.B COMSPEC
and
.B SYSTEMROOT
become forward slashes, as busybox-w32 does; otherwise, as from a
native mode shell, the environment is taken as is (see Environment
below).
.TP
environment for programs
In msys mode the path variables are converted back to Windows form for
programs, and for a Windows program that is not an MSYS2 program, other
values that look like POSIX paths are converted too.
In native mode programs get the environment unchanged.
.TP
argument conversion
In msys mode arguments to a Windows program, other than an MSYS2
program, that look like POSIX paths are converted to Windows paths by
MSYS2's rules, unless
.B MSYS_NO_PATHCONV
or
.B MSYS2_ARG_CONV_EXCL
says otherwise (see Running programs below).
In native mode arguments are passed as written, except for libtool's
.B "cmd //c echo"
(see Running programs below).
.TP
command lookup and extensions
In msys mode a command
.B foo
finds
.IR foo.exe ,
and
.BR type ,
.BR "command \-v" ,
and
.B hash
show it without the
.BR .exe .
In native mode
.B foo
is tried as written and then with
.BR .com ,
.BR .exe ,
.BR .sh ,
.BR .bat ,
and
.BR .cmd ,
and the name shown includes the extension (see Finding commands below).
.TP
what is executable
In msys mode a file is executable when its name ends in
.B .exe
or it begins with
.BR #! ,
.BR MZ ,
or a line holding only
.B :
and ending in a bare LF; other names, such as
.B .bat
or
.BR .com ,
count for nothing.
In native mode a file is executable when its name ends in one of the
five extensions above, or it begins with
.BR #! ,
or it is a Windows program.
.TP
carriage returns
In msys mode the parser drops every carriage return, and
.B set \-o igncr
also drops them from
.B read
and command substitutions.
In native mode the parser and
.B read
drop a carriage return only before a newline.
In both modes a command substitution strips trailing newlines with one
carriage return before each.
.TP
startup files
In msys mode the files are bash's, plus
.I /etc/bash.bashrc
and
.IR /etc/bash.bash_logout .
In native mode they follow busybox-w32's rules: profile files for a login
shell and the file named by
.B ENV
for an interactive one (see Startup files above).
.TP
.BR OSTYPE " and " MACHTYPE
In msys mode
.B OSTYPE
is
.B cygwin
and
.B MACHTYPE
is
.BR x86_64\-pc\-cygwin .
In native mode they are
.B win32
and
.BR x86_64\-w64\-mingw32 .
A 32-bit build has
.B i686
in place of
.BR x86_64 .
On other systems they name the build's target as bash's configure
would, such as
.B linux\-gnu
and
.BR aarch64\-unknown\-linux\-gnu ,
or on macOS
.B darwin
and
.B aarch64\-apple\-darwin
with the kernel release appended.
.TP
POSIX mode
In msys mode a shell named
.BR sh ,
possible only in an installation with an
.IR etc/fstab ,
enters POSIX mode after its startup files.
In native mode the name never selects POSIX mode.
.TP
busybox applets
In msys mode an applet's alias program runs like any other Windows
program.
In native mode an alias program found beside
.B busybox.exe
is skipped and
.B busybox.exe
runs the applet directly, unless
.B BB_OVERRIDE_APPLETS
names it (see Running programs below).
.TP
devices
In msys mode
.I /dev
holds Cygwin's usual names and
.I /proc
a few entries.
In native mode only
.IR /dev/null ,
.IR /dev/zero ,
.IR /dev/urandom ,
.IR /dev/clipboard ,
.IR /dev/tty ,
.IR /dev/console ,
.IR /dev/stdin ,
.IR /dev/stdout ,
.IR /dev/stderr ,
and
.I /dev/fd/N
exist, and a DOS device name such as
.B nul
is a device in any directory and with any extension (see Devices below).
.TP
.BR cygpath " and " "pwd \-W"
In msys mode
.B cygpath
is a builtin and
.B pwd \-W
prints the Windows form of the working directory.
In native mode there is no
.B cygpath
builtin, and
.B pwd \-W
prints the same as
.BR pwd .
.SS "Paths in msys mode"
Paths are POSIX paths over a mount table, which maps POSIX directories
onto Windows directories.
The install root is as defined under Names and modes above.
.PP
Without an
.I etc/fstab
in the install root, as in w64devkit, the table is:
.IP \(bu 2
.B /
is the install root.
.IP \(bu 2
.I /bin
is the shell's own directory when the shell sits in
.IR <root>/bin ,
as in w64devkit, and
.I <root>/usr/bin
otherwise.
.I /usr/bin
is always
.I <root>/usr/bin
through the root mount, and does not exist in w64devkit.
.IP \(bu 2
.I /tmp
is the directory named by
.BR TEMP ,
or by
.B TMP
when
.B TEMP
is unset, as found in the environment at startup.
.IP \(bu 2
.IR /c ,
.IR /d ,
and so on are the drives: the drive prefix is empty.
A first component that is a single letter always names a drive, even
when the root holds a directory of that name.
Drives are not listed in
.BR / .
.IP \(bu 2
.I /dev
and
.I /proc
are virtual directories (see Devices below).
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ cygpath \-m / /bin /tmp /c
C:/w64devkit/
C:/w64devkit/bin
C:/Users/me/AppData/Local/Temp
C:/
.EE
.RE
.PP
An
.I etc/fstab
replaces those defaults, as in an MSYS2 or Git for Windows installation,
and selects msys mode for every name (see Names and modes above).
Only that one file is read.
Each line holds a Windows directory, a POSIX mount point, a type, and
options, separated by blanks or tabs, and further fields are ignored.
In a field
.B \e040
is a space, a line whose first field begins with
.B #
is a comment, and a carriage return ending a line is ignored.
For an ordinary mount the Windows directory must be a drive path or a
UNC path, and the mount point must begin with
.BR / ,
or the line is ignored.
A line with only two fields mounts the directory with the mount point
matched ignoring case, a line with three fields is ignored, and so is a
line with an option not in this list:
.BR acl ,
.BR auto ,
.BR binary ,
.BR bind ,
.BR cygexec ,
.BR dos ,
.BR exec ,
.BR ihash ,
.BR noacl ,
.BR nosuid ,
.BR notexec ,
.BR nouser ,
.BR override ,
.BR posix=0 ,
.BR posix=1 ,
.BR sparse ,
.BR text ,
and
.BR user .
Only four of them have an effect:
.TP
.B posix=0
Match the mount point ignoring case.
.TP
.B posix=1
Match the mount point exactly, the default for a line with options.
.TP
.B bind
The first field is a POSIX path, resolved through the mounts made so
far.
.TP
.B override
Replace the
.B /
mount or a
.B usertemp
mount, which other lines cannot replace.
.PP
The type
.B cygdrive
sets the drive prefix to the mount point and ignores the first field, so
.B "none / cygdrive binary,posix=0 0 0"
gives
.I /c
as in Git for Windows.
A mount point that does not begin with
.B /
sets the prefix to
.IR / .
The type
.B usertemp
mounts the
.B TEMP
(else
.BR TMP )
directory at the mount point.
Any other type is an ordinary mount.
A later line for the same mount point replaces an earlier one.
With an
.IR etc/fstab ,
the drive prefix is
.I /cygdrive/
unless a
.B cygdrive
line sets it,
.I /bin
is
.I <root>/usr/bin
unless a line mounts
.IR /bin ,
and there is no
.I /tmp
unless a line mounts it.
.PP
A POSIX path maps through the drive prefix first: a path below the
prefix names a drive or nothing, and with a prefix of
.I /
a single-letter first component names a drive even when a line mounts
it.
Any other path maps through the mount point that is the longest match at
a component boundary.
The default
.I /bin
mount, the drive prefix,
.IR /dev ,
and
.I /proc
match case-sensitively, while the default
.I /tmp
mount and the drive letter ignore case.
A directory listing, for globbing or completion, also shows the mount
points directly inside an existing directory, and
.B /
lists
.I dev
and
.IR proc .
.PP
Windows paths are accepted wherever a path is opened, tested, or given
to
.BR cd :
.IR C:/x ,
.IR C:\ex ,
.I \ex
on the current drive,
.I //server/share
and
.IR \e\eserver\eshare ,
and paths with a
.I \e\e?\e
prefix, so
.I \e\e?\eC:\ex
is
.IR /c/x .
A bare
.I C:
is the root of the drive and
.I C:x
is
.IR C:/x ,
not a path under the drive's working directory.
A relative path is joined to
.B PWD
first, so
.B ..\&
climbs in the POSIX namespace, across mount points.
.PP
.BR pwd ,
.BR PWD ,
the prompt, and
.B \(ti
give POSIX paths.
A Windows path is shown through the mount whose Windows directory holds
it, chosen by Cygwin's rule (the largest POSIX length minus Windows length
wins), so a directory under
.B TEMP
shows as
.IR /tmp/... ,
and otherwise a drive path shows under the drive prefix with a lowercase
letter.
The Windows directory is compared with the same case rule as the mount
point, so a path spelled in another case than the root or
.I /bin
mount shows under the drive prefix instead.
Globbing keeps the spelling of the pattern's directory part, and
.B cd
keeps the case typed.
.B cd
converts an absolute operand written in Windows form, so
.B cd C:\eWindows
gives
.IR /c/Windows ,
but leaves a drive-relative one in Windows form, so
.B cd C:Windows
sets
.B PWD
to
.IR C:/Windows .
An inherited
.B PWD
that names the working directory is kept as it is spelled, even in
Windows form: a bash started from native
.BR sh ,
which exports
.BR PWD=C:/Users/me ,
shows
.I C:/Users/me
until
.B cd
is given an absolute path.
.PP
.BR PATH ,
.BR CDPATH ,
and the other lists are split on every colon, so a Windows path in one
is split at its drive letter: write
.IR /c/x ,
not
.IR C:/x .
.PP
.B pwd \-W
prints the working directory in Windows form with forward slashes and no
trailing slash except on a drive root:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ cd \(aqC:\eWindows\(aq
$ pwd
/c/Windows
$ pwd \-W
C:/Windows
$ cygpath \-w /tmp/x
C:\eUsers\eme\eAppData\eLocal\eTemp\ex
.EE
.RE
.PP
The
.B cygpath
builtin converts each
.I name
between the POSIX form, the Windows form with backslashes, and the mixed
form with forward slashes, through the shell's own mount table:
.PP
.RS 4
.B cygpath
.RB [ \-u | \-w | \-m ]
.RB [ \-alps ]
.IR name ...
.PP
.B cygpath
.RB [ \-u | \-w | \-m ]
.RB [ \-alps ]
.B \-f
.I file
.PP
.B cygpath
.RB [ \-u | \-w | \-m ]
.BR \-W | \-S
.RE
.PP
Short options may be grouped, and
.B \-\-
ends the options.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-unix
Print the POSIX form, the default.
A relative name stays relative, with
.B \e
turned into
.BR / .
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-windows
Print the Windows form with backslashes.
A relative name stays relative unless
.B ..\&
climbs above the working directory.
A name with no Windows form, such as
.I /dev/tty
or
.IR /proc/self ,
is printed as given;
.IR /dev/null ,
the generator devices, and
.I /dev/clipboard
print as
.IR \e\e.\eNUL ,
and
.I /dev/fd
prints as
.BI \eproc\e pid \efd\fR,
with the shell's process ID.
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-mixed
Print the Windows form with forward slashes.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-absolute
Make a relative name absolute, against the working directory.
Ignored with
.BR \-p .
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-path
Convert lists.
With
.BR \-u ,
a list is split on
.B ;
and joined with
.BR : ,
dropping empty elements.
With
.B \-w
or
.BR \-m ,
a list is split on every
.B :
and joined with
.BR ; ,
and an empty element becomes
.BR . .
.TP
\fB\-f\fR \fIfile\fR, \fB\-\-file\fR[\fB=\fR]\fIfile\fR
Convert each line of
.IR file ,
or of standard input when
.I file
is
.BR \- .
The argument may also be attached to
.BR \-f .
A carriage return ending a line is dropped.
.TP
.BR \-W ", " \-\-windir
Print the Windows directory:
.BR SYSTEMROOT ,
else
.BR WINDIR ,
else
.IR C:/Windows .
.TP
.BR \-S ", " \-\-sysdir
Print the Windows system directory, the Windows directory's
.IR System32 .
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-long\-name ", " \-s ", " \-\-short\-name
Accepted with no effect.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary on standard output and exit 0.
.PP
The exit status is 0 on success and 1 on an error.
An unknown option, a missing
.I file
for
.BR \-f ,
more than one of
.BR \-u ,
.BR \-w ,
and
.BR \-m ,
a name given with
.B \-W
or
.BR \-S ,
or no names print a message and the usage line on standard error.
An empty
.I name
stops the conversion with
.BR "can\(aqt convert empty path" .
.B help
has no
.B cygpath
topic, as in Git Bash, where
.B cygpath
is a program: use
.BR "cygpath \-\-help" .
A copy of the program named
.I cygpath.exe
acts as this builtin and exits.
.PP
The parser drops every carriage return in msys mode: in scripts,
.B \-c
text,
.BR eval ,
sourced files, command substitution bodies, and interactive lines,
including those inside quotes and here-documents.
.B $\(aq\er\(aq
still gives a carriage return.
Without
.BR igncr ,
.B read
and
.B mapfile
keep carriage returns, as in Git Bash.
.PP
.B igncr
is a
.B set \-o
option that exists only in msys mode, with no single-letter flag, and is
off by default; it may also come from an inherited
.BR SHELLOPTS .
When it is on, command substitutions lose every carriage return, and
.B read
drops them from what it stores, not counting them for
.B \-n
or
.BR \-N ;
a carriage return escaped with
.B \e
stays.
.B mapfile
keeps them.
.PP
In both Windows modes a command substitution strips trailing newlines
together with one carriage return before each, so
.B a
CR LF CR LF gives
.BR a ,
while
.B a
CR CR LF keeps one carriage return and a lone trailing carriage return
stays.
.SS "Paths in native mode"
Paths are Windows paths, as busybox-w32's shell has them.
There is no mount table.
A path beginning with a single
.B /
or
.B \e
is on the root of the current drive, or of the current share when the
working directory is a UNC path.
.I /c/x
is
.I C:/c/x
when the working directory is on drive C, not
.IR C:/x .
.I /proc
is an ordinary directory, and of
.I /dev
only the names under Devices below are special.
.PP
.I C:/x
and
.I C:\ex
are absolute.
.I C:
and
.I C:x
are relative to drive C's working directory, which comes from the
.B =C:
environment entry that Windows programs inherit, else from the working
directory when it is on drive C, else the drive root.
Each
.BR cd ,
in either mode, records the new directory in that drive's entry, which
child processes inherit.
UNC paths are written
.I //server/share
or
.IR \e\eserver\eshare ,
and a
.I \e\e?\e
prefix is accepted, so
.I \e\e?\eC:\ex
is
.IR C:/x .
Both slashes separate components, and
.B .\&
and
.B ..\&
are resolved as text.
.PP
.B pwd
and
.B PWD
give Windows paths with forward slashes and an uppercase drive letter,
such as
.IR C:/Users/me .
.B \(ti
is
.B HOME
as it stands: the shell does not make it canonical, and it has forward
slashes only where the import or the default from
.B USERPROFILE
gave them (see Environment below).
An inherited
.B PWD
is never used: at startup
.B PWD
is the process's working directory.
.B cd
gives each component the case it has on disk, so
.B cd c:/users/ME
shows
.IR C:/Users/me ,
but a share's server and share names keep the case typed.
Globbing does no conversion: matches keep the spelling of the pattern's
directory part.
In a pattern, as in msys mode, a backslash quotes the next character, so
patterns need forward slashes.
.PP
.B PATH
and
.B CDPATH
are split on
.BR ; .
Every assignment to
.BR PATH ,
and its import at startup, turns each colon into a semicolon, except a
colon after a single letter at the start of an element, which is taken
for a drive letter (so
.I a:b
stays).
.B CDPATH
is not rewritten.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ PATH=C:/x:/usr/bin:D:/y
$ echo "$PATH"
C:/x;/usr/bin;D:/y
.EE
.RE
.PP
For libtool, when a Windows program is run as exactly
.BR "cmd //c echo" ,
the
.B //c
is passed as
.BR /c .
Other spellings, such as
.BR cmd.exe ,
are left alone.
Msys mode gets the same effect from its argument conversion.
.PP
The parser drops a carriage return that comes before a newline, so
scripts with CR LF line endings run, but keeps one elsewhere, even
inside quotes.
.B read
drops a carriage return before the newline, except with
.BR \-N ,
and field splitting turns CR LF into LF whatever
.B IFS
holds.
.B mapfile
keeps carriage returns, and a command substitution strips trailing
newlines with one carriage return before each, as in msys mode.
There is no
.B igncr
option.
.PP
Some busybox-w32 features are missing.
Globbing is case sensitive, as in bash, unless
.B shopt \-s nocaseglob
is set, where busybox-w32 ignores case by default.
There is no
.B \-X
option, no
.B title
builtin, and no
.BR "cd \-a" ,
and the colons in
.B MANPATH
are not rewritten.
Of the
.B BB_
variables only
.B BB_OVERRIDE_APPLETS
is read:
.BR BB_GLOBBING ,
.BR BB_UMASK ,
.BR BB_SYSTEMROOT ,
and the others have no effect.
.SS Devices
The shell emulates devices in its own redirections, file tests,
pathname expansion, and
.BR cd .
A program given a device name as an argument sees what argument
conversion makes of it (see Running programs below).
In msys mode
.I /dev/null
becomes
.B nul
and
.I /dev/zero
becomes
.IR /Device/Null ,
which busybox's applets cannot open, so redirect instead:
.BR "head \-c 4 </dev/urandom" .
In native mode the name is passed as written, and busybox's applets
emulate it themselves.
.PP
In msys mode
.I /dev
is a virtual directory that lists
.IR clipboard ,
.IR console ,
.IR fd ,
.IR full ,
.IR null ,
.IR random ,
.IR stderr ,
.IR stdin ,
.IR stdout ,
.IR tty ,
.IR urandom ,
and
.IR zero .
.IR ptmx ,
.IR conin ,
.IR conout ,
.IR windows ,
.IR consN ,
.IR ptyN ,
and
.IR ttySN ,
for a number
.IR N ,
are recognised but not listed.
The names are matched case-sensitively.
.TP
.I /dev/null
The NUL device.
.TP
.IR /dev/tty ", " /dev/console
The console, opened as
.B CONIN$
for reading and
.B CONOUT$
otherwise.
.TP
.IR /dev/stdin ", " /dev/stdout ", " /dev/stderr ", " /dev/fd/N
A copy of the shell's descriptor 0, 1, 2, or
.IR N .
A closed descriptor gives
.BR "Bad file descriptor" .
.I /dev/fd
is a directory that cannot be listed.
.TP
.IR /dev/zero ", " /dev/full
Read as endless zero bytes.
Writes to
.I /dev/zero
are discarded.
Writes to
.I /dev/full
fail: the shell's own with
.BR "No space left on device" ,
a program's because its handle is open only for reading.
.TP
.IR /dev/random ", " /dev/urandom
Read as the same endless stream of random bytes, from ChaCha8 keyed from
the operating system's random source.
Writes are discarded.
.TP
.I /dev/clipboard
The Windows clipboard's text.
Opened for reading, it is a snapshot of the text as it is then, line
endings unchanged (text copied from most Windows programs ends its lines
with CRLF); a clipboard that holds no text, or only an image, reads as
empty.
Opened for writing, with
.B >
or
.BR >> ,
it starts empty, and what is written through that open accumulates:
each write by the shell sets the clipboard to all of it, and a program's
output is added when the program closes it.
An open with no write, such as
.BR :\~>/dev/clipboard ,
leaves the clipboard alone.
While another program keeps the clipboard open, the shell retries for
a few hundred milliseconds at most and then fails with
.BR "Permission denied" .
Only the shell's own redirections reach it, so write
.BR "cat </dev/clipboard" ,
not
.BR "cat /dev/clipboard" .
.TP
The names recognised but not listed
Writes are discarded and reads fail with
.BR "No such file or directory" .
.PP
A generator device opened for reading is a pipe that the shell fills, so
builtins and programs read it alike, and
.B test \-p
is true of its descriptor.
A path below a device, such as
.IR /dev/null/x ,
fails with
.BR "Not a directory" .
There is no
.I /dev/tcp
or
.IR /dev/udp .
.PP
In msys mode
.I /proc
lists Cygwin's fixed entries
.RI ( cpuinfo ,
.IR mounts ,
.IR version ,
and the rest), the shell's own process ID, and
.IR self .
The fixed files cannot be read
.RB ( "Permission denied" )
and the fixed directories list nothing.
Only these have contents:
.TP
.I /proc/self/exename
The shell's POSIX path, without
.BR .exe .
.TP
.I /proc/self/winexename
The shell's Windows path, with backslashes.
.TP
.I /proc/self/fd/N
The shell's descriptor
.IR N ,
as
.IR /dev/fd/N .
.TP
.I /proc/cygdrive
The drive prefix, so
.I /proc/cygdrive/c
is drive C.
.PP
.I /proc/
followed by
.B $$
or
.BR BASHPID ,
even in a subshell, is the same as
.IR /proc/self ,
so
.BI /proc/$BASHPID/fd/ N
names the shell's own descriptor
.IR N .
These paths work only in the shell's own redirections and builtins: a
program such as busybox
.B cat
cannot open them.
.B cd
can enter
.I /dev
and
.IR /proc ;
a program started there runs in the install root.
.PP
In native mode, as in busybox-w32, only
.IR /dev/null ,
.IR /dev/tty ,
.IR /dev/console ,
.IR /dev/stdin ,
.IR /dev/stdout ,
.IR /dev/stderr ,
.IR /dev/fd/N ,
.IR /dev/zero ,
and
.I /dev/urandom
are devices, and so is
.IR /dev/clipboard ,
which busybox-w32 lacks; all behave as in msys mode.
Any other name, such as
.IR /dev/full ,
.IR /dev/random ,
or
.I /dev/fd
itself, is an ordinary path on the current drive, though a path below
one of the msys names, such as
.IR /dev/random/x ,
still fails with
.BR "Not a directory" .
.PP
In both Windows modes the DOS device names
.BR CON ,
.BR PRN ,
.BR AUX ,
.BR NUL ,
.BR COM1 " to " COM9 ,
.BR LPT1 " to " LPT9 ,
.BR CONIN$ ,
and
.BR CONOUT$ ,
in any case and in any directory, open the device, so
.B 2>nul
discards standard error where Git Bash would create a file named
.IR nul .
.B NUL
is
.I /dev/null
and
.B CON
the console.
In native mode the Windows rules apply: an extension, anything after a
colon, and trailing dots and spaces are ignored, so
.I nul.txt
and
.I con:
are devices too.
In msys mode only the bare name is a device, and a name such as
.IR nul.txt ,
or one ending in a dot or a space, is an ordinary file that can be
created.
.SS Environment
At startup every environment entry whose name is a valid variable name
becomes an exported variable.
An entry whose name is not, such as
.B ProgramFiles(x86)
or the
.B =C:
entries that hold each drive's working directory, defines no variable
but is passed on to programs, converted only as described below for a
native program started from msys mode.
An entry named
.BI BASH_FUNC_ name %%
defines the exported function
.IR name ,
as in bash, and reaches programs only while that function stays
exported.
Native mode converts some environments as it imports them, as
described below.
.PP
In msys mode the import converts as an MSYS2 program's runtime does.
Names that match
.BR COMMONPROGRAMFILES ,
.BR COMSPEC ,
.BR PATH ,
.BR PROGRAMFILES ,
.BR SYSTEMDRIVE ,
.BR SYSTEMROOT ,
.BR TEMP ,
.BR TMP ,
.BR WINDIR ,
or
.B MSYSTEM
ignoring case are uppercased, so
.B Path
becomes
.BR PATH .
.B PATH
and
.B ORIGINAL_PATH
are split on
.BR ; ,
each element loses surrounding double quotes and trailing backslashes,
empty elements are dropped, and the rest are converted to POSIX form and
joined with
.BR : .
.B LD_LIBRARY_PATH
is converted the same way, but its elements keep their quotes and
trailing backslashes.
.BR HOME ,
.BR SHELL ,
.BR TMPDIR ,
.BR TMP ,
and
.B TEMP
are converted to POSIX form, a relative value joined to the working
directory.
An inherited
.B TERM=msys
is dropped.
Other values are kept as they are.
When unset,
.B HOME
is taken from
.B USERPROFILE
in POSIX form,
.B TERM
is set to
.BR xterm\-256color ,
and
.B PATH
to
.IR /usr/bin ,
which names no directory in w64devkit's layout; all three are exported.
.B SHELL
defaults to
.I /usr/bin/bash
and is not exported.
.PP
A native program, or a batch file, started from msys mode gets the
environment converted back, as MSYS2 builds it for a Windows program:
.IP \(bu 2
.BR PATH ,
.BR ORIGINAL_PATH ,
and
.B LD_LIBRARY_PATH
become Windows lists: split on every
.BR : ,
an empty element becoming
.BR . ,
each element converted to Windows form with backslashes, and joined
with
.BR ; .
.IP \(bu 2
.BR HOME ,
.BR SHELL ,
.BR TMPDIR ,
.BR TMP ,
and
.B TEMP
become absolute Windows paths with backslashes.
When such a value names no file but the same name with
.B .exe
added is a file,
.B .exe
is appended, so
.B SHELL=/bin/bash
arrives as the full path of
.IR bash.exe .
These eight names are matched exactly, in uppercase.
.IP \(bu 2
Every other entry with a non-empty value goes, whole, through the
argument conversion described under Running programs below, so
.B A=/c/x
arrives as
.BR A=C:/x .
No entry does when the environment holds
.BR MSYS_NO_PATHCONV ,
and an entry is skipped when it starts with one of the
.BR ; -separated
prefixes in
.B MSYS2_ENV_CONV_EXCL
(whose name is matched ignoring case), or when that variable holds
.BR * .
.IP \(bu 2
.B TZ
(in any case) is dropped unless its value is empty, or does not start
with
.B :
and holds only letters, digits,
.BR \- ,
.BR + ,
and
.BR : .
So
.B TZ=EST5EDT
passes, while
.B EST5EDT,M3.2.0,M11.1.0
and
.B America/New_York
do not.
.IP \(bu 2
The
.B =C:
entries are passed unchanged.
When
.B PATH
is not exported or is empty (an empty
.B PATH=
entry stays too),
.BI PATH= root \eusr
is added, where
.I root
is the install root in Windows form.
.B SYSTEMROOT
and
.B WINDIR
are added from the Windows directory when missing.
.B MSYSTEM
is never added.
.PP
For example:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ export A=/c/x B=\-\-opt=/c/y C=/c/a:/d/b
$ printenv A B C
C:/x
\-\-opt=C:/y
C:\ea;D:\eb
$ MSYS2_ENV_CONV_EXCL=B printenv A B
C:/x
\-\-opt=/c/y
.EE
.RE
.PP
An MSYS2 or Cygwin program, or a copy of this shell, started from msys
mode gets only the eight variables above in Windows form, by the same
rules, and every other entry as it is.
An MSYS2 or Cygwin program converts them back at startup, as does a
copy in msys mode.
A copy in native mode imports them as native mode does (see below):
their backslashes become
.B /
when the environment holds a mixed-case
.BR ProgramData ,
as it does when Windows started the msys mode shell, and stay
otherwise.
A Windows-form value in one of them therefore reaches such a program in
POSIX form, and
.B MSYS2_ENV_CONV_EXCL
and
.B MSYS_NO_PATHCONV
do not prevent that.
An MSYS2 program converts with its own install's mount table, not the
shell's.
.PP
In native mode the import follows busybox-w32, but only when the
environment holds an entry named exactly
.BR Path ,
.BR ComSpec ,
or
.BR ProgramData ,
as one from a Windows program, Git Bash, or an msys mode shell does.
Then every name is uppercased, and every value except those of
.B COMSPEC
and
.B SYSTEMROOT
has its backslashes turned into
.BR / .
A name that is not a valid variable name is passed on to programs
unchanged and also defines a variable with each invalid character
replaced by
.BR _ ,
unless the result starts with a digit:
.B ProgramFiles(x86)
gives
.BR PROGRAMFILES_X86_ .
From another native shell or busybox, whose names are already
uppercase, the environment is imported as it is.
Either way, when unset,
.B HOME
is taken from
.B USERPROFILE
with forward slashes,
.B USER
and
.B LOGNAME
from
.BR USERNAME ,
.B PATH
is the shell's own directory, and
.B SHELL
is the shell's own path; none of these is exported.
.B TERM
is left unset.
Native mode passes the environment to every program as it stands,
without conversion, so imported values keep their forward slashes.
.SS "Finding commands"
A command name that contains
.B /
or
.BR \e ,
or starts with a drive such as
.BR C: ,
is a path and is not looked up in
.BR PATH .
Otherwise the directories in
.B PATH
are searched in order, an empty element meaning the working directory.
.B PATH
is split on
.B :
in msys mode and on
.B ;
in native mode.
With
.B PATH
unset, only the bare name in the working directory is tried, and a name
not found there gives
.IB name ": No such file or directory"
and status 127.
.B command \-p
searches
.I /usr/bin:/bin
in msys mode and the shell's own directory in native mode.
.PP
In msys mode a name
.I foo
that does not exist is found as
.I foo.exe
when that is a file, and no other suffix is tried: a batch file must be
named in full, as in
.BR build.bat .
The same holds for a path such as
.IR ./foo ,
and wherever the shell tests a file name, so
.B [ \-e ls ]
is true in a directory that holds only
.IR ls.exe .
.BR type ,
.BR "command \-v" ,
and
.B hash
show the path without
.BR .exe .
.PP
In native mode each directory is tried for the bare name and then for
the name with
.BR .com ,
.BR .exe ,
.BR .sh ,
.BR .bat ,
and
.B .cmd
appended, in that order, unless the name already ends in one of those
(in any case) or in a dot.
A path that does not exist and lacks such an extension gets the same
five tried, and a script found this way runs under the name with the
extension appended:
.B ./s
runs
.I s.sh
with
.B $0
set to
.BR ./s.sh .
.B PATHEXT
is not read, nor are
.B MSYS
and
.BR CYGWIN ,
in either mode, so settings such as
.B noglob
in them have no effect.
.B type
and
.B command \-v
show the file found on
.BR PATH ,
extension included, but find nothing for a path such as
.I ./foo
that runs only with an extension added.
.PP
Which files are executable, for
.B test \-x
and for the search, depends on the mode.
In msys mode, as Cygwin decides without ACLs, a regular file is
executable when its name ends in
.BR .exe ,
or its content starts with
.BR #! ,
.BR MZ ,
or a colon and a bare LF.
Other names, such as
.BR .bat ,
.BR .cmd ,
or
.BR .com ,
count for nothing, so a batch file or an empty file is not executable,
while a
.B .com
file that is a Windows image is.
In native mode, as busybox-w32 decides, a file is executable when its
name ends in one of the five extensions above, or it starts with
.BR #! ,
or it is an Actually Portable Executable (starting
.BR MZqFpD ),
or it is a Windows image that is not a DLL and has the native, GUI,
console, or POSIX subsystem.
.PP
The search takes the first executable match.
When nothing executable matches, it takes the first other file found,
as bash does.
Running it then fails in native mode with
.B "Permission denied"
and status 126, as in busybox-w32.
In msys mode it runs anyway, by the rules under Running programs below,
as under Git Bash; this is how a batch file named in full runs.
As in bash, a found path is remembered
.RB ( hash );
assigning
.B PATH
forgets every remembered path, as does switching modes with the hidden
.B msyspath
option.
.PP
w64devkit installs each busybox applet, such as
.I ls.exe
or
.IR sed.exe ,
as a small stub program that starts
.IR busybox.exe .
In native mode the shell skips such a stub, whether found on
.B PATH
or named by path: when the program it is about to run is a Windows
image of at most 64 KiB that uses no C runtime, is not a DLL, an MSYS2
or Cygwin program, or the shell itself, and contains the text
.BR busybox.exe ,
and a
.I busybox.exe
is in the same directory, the shell starts that
.I busybox.exe
directly, passing the stub's name less
.B .exe
as the first argument, which names the applet, then the other
arguments as given, with the environment unchanged.
.B BB_OVERRIDE_APPLETS
names applets whose stubs run as ordinary programs instead.
It is read from the exported environment.
Its entries are separated by spaces, commas, or semicolons (not tabs),
a leading
.B +
on an entry is ignored, names are compared ignoring case, and an entry
.B \-
means every applet.
Msys mode always runs the stub itself, as any other native program.
.SS "Running programs"
The shell starts each program itself with
.BR CreateProcess ,
not through
.B cmd.exe
or another shell; only a batch file runs through
.BR cmd.exe ,
as described below.
The shell process never changes its own Windows working directory:
.B cd
changes the directory the shell keeps, and each program starts there.
The directory the shell was started in therefore cannot be renamed or
removed while the shell runs, even after
.BR cd .
In msys mode a program started from a virtual directory such as
.I /proc
starts in the install root.
When Windows refuses the directory, as when it is too long or has been
removed, the program starts in the directory the shell itself was
started in.
.PP
A file whose name ends in
.B .bat
or
.B .cmd
(in any case) runs as a batch file, whatever it holds.
Otherwise the file's first bytes decide.
A Windows image that imports
.I msys\-2.0.dll
or
.I cygwin1.dll
is an MSYS2 or Cygwin program, an image of this shell under any name is
a copy of the shell, and any other image is a native program.
A file starting with
.B #!\&
is a script.
.PP
Windows passes a program one command line, which the program splits
into arguments, so the shell quotes each argument for the way the
target splits:
.TP
Native program, native mode
By the rules of MSVCRT and
.BR CommandLineToArgvW :
an argument is put in double quotes when it is empty or contains a
space, tab, newline, vertical tab,
.BR \(dq ,
.BR * ,
.BR ? ,
.BR [ ,
.BR { ,
or
.BR \(aq ,
and inside the quotes a
.B \(dq
is escaped with a backslash and the backslashes before it, or before
the closing quote, are doubled.
Quoting the wildcards keeps a program that expands its own command line
from expanding them again.
The first argument has its slashes turned into backslashes, so
.B ./prog
reaches the program as
.BR .\eprog .
.TP
Native program, msys mode
As an MSYS2 parent quotes, which every MSVCRT parser reads the same way:
the first argument is the program's full Windows path with backslashes,
the others are converted as described below, and an argument is quoted
only when it is empty or contains a space, tab, carriage return,
newline, or
.BR \(dq .
Since wildcards are not quoted, a program that expands its own command
line expands an argument such as
.B \(aq*.c\(aq
itself, as under Git Bash.
.TP
MSYS2 or Cygwin program
By Cygwin's rules, in both modes: an argument is quoted when it is
empty, starts with
.B ~
or
.BR @ ,
or contains a space, tab, newline, carriage return,
.BR \(dq ,
.BR \(aq ,
.BR ? ,
.BR * ,
.BR [ ,
.BR ( ,
.BR ) ,
.BR { ,
or
.BR } ,
and inside the quotes every
.B \e
and
.B \(dq
gets a backslash.
The program receives each argument unchanged: none is converted, and
the program expands no wildcard or response file in it.
.TP
Copy of this shell
Quoted for the mode the copy's own name and install give it: Cygwin's
rules for msys mode, MSVCRT's for native mode.
.TP
Batch file
Run through
.B cmd.exe
with arguments escaped for it, as described next.
.PP
A batch file runs as exactly
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
%COMSPEC% /d /e:on /v:off /s /c "\fIfile\fR \fIargs\fR & exit"
.EE
.RE
.PP
where
.B COMSPEC
comes from the program's environment, ignoring case, and defaults to
.I cmd.exe
in the System32 directory, and
.I file
is the batch file's Windows path, always quoted.
The trailing
.B exit
keeps the status of
.BR "exit /b" ,
which the
.B cmd.exe
of Windows XP otherwise loses.
Each argument is escaped so that
.B cmd.exe
takes it literally, as Rust does against the flaw known as BatBadBut:
it is quoted when it is empty, ends in a backslash, or holds anything
but letters, digits, non-ASCII characters, and
.BR #$*+\-./:?@\e_ ,
inside the quotes a
.B \(dq
is doubled, as are the backslashes before it and at the end, and every
.B %
is broken up so that no variable is expanded.
An argument containing a carriage return or a newline cannot be passed,
and the command fails with
.IB name ": Invalid argument"
and status 126, where
.I name
is the command as typed.
In msys mode the arguments are first converted as for a native program,
and the environment is the native program's.
.PP
A command line longer than 32766 UTF-16 code units fails with
.B "Argument list too long"
and status 126.
A program that requires elevation, such as many installers, is not
elevated: it fails with
.B "Permission denied"
and status 126.
Run it from an elevated shell, or through the
.B start
command of
.BR cmd.exe .
In native mode
.B "cmd //c echo"
is rewritten as described under Paths in native mode above.
When a native program fails to start because a DLL is missing, the
shell prints, as Cygwin does,
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
\fIprog\fR: error while loading shared libraries: \fIdll\fR: cannot
open shared object file: No such file or directory
.EE
.RE
.PP
on one line, and the status is 127.
.I prog
is in POSIX form in msys mode, and
.I dll
is the first import found in none of the program's directory, the
System32, SysWOW64, and Windows directories, and the program's
.BR PATH ,
or
.B ?\&
if they are all found.
.PP
In msys mode each argument but the first to a native program or batch
file goes through MSYS2's path conversion, which turns what looks like
a POSIX path into a Windows path.
The shell copies MSYS2's rules, including their mistakes:
.IP \(bu 2
An argument is examined only when it contains
.B /
or
.B \e
before its first blank.
An empty argument is never converted.
.IP \(bu 2
An argument is left whole when it starts with
.B ~
or
.BR : ,
or contains anywhere a backquote,
.BR \(aq ,
.BR * ,
.BR ? ,
.BR [ ,
.BR ] ,
.BR /~ ,
.BR :: ,
.BR :./ ,
.BR :../ ,
or
.BR @@ .
.IP \(bu 2
Leading characters other than letters, digits,
.BR / ,
.BR \e ,
.BR : ,
.BR \- ,
and
.B .\&
are kept, and conversion starts after them, so a response file
.B @/c/args
becomes
.BR @C:/args .
.IP \(bu 2
A rooted path is converted through the mount table, with forward
slashes:
.B /c/Users/me
becomes
.BR C:/Users/me ,
and with w64devkit at
.IR C:/w64devkit ,
.B /usr/include
becomes
.BR C:/w64devkit/usr/include .
.B /dev/null
becomes
.BR nul ,
.IR /dev/zero ,
.IR /dev/full ,
.IR /dev/random ,
.IR /dev/urandom ,
and
.I /dev/clipboard
become
.IR /Device/Null ,
and
.I /dev/stdin
becomes
.IR /proc/self/fd/0 ,
likewise for standard output and error,
.I /dev/fd
becomes
.IR /proc/self/fd ,
and
.BI /dev/fd/ n
becomes
.BI /proc/ pid /fd/ n
with the shell's process ID.
Conversion stops at the first
.BR /.. ,
and the rest is copied as it is.
Any other path with no Windows form, such as
.IR /dev/tty ,
is left as typed.
.IP \(bu 2
A rooted path whose first
.B :
is followed by
.B /
or
.BR . ,
and a word such as
.BR a/b:/c ,
are path lists: each element is converted on its own, with
backslashes, and the elements are joined with
.BR ; .
.IP \(bu 2
Conversion also starts after the first
.BR = ,
as in
.B \-\-prefix=/usr
or
.BR VAR=/x ;
right after an option letter followed by
.BR / ,
as in
.B \-I/usr/include
or
.BR \-L/lib ;
and after a
.B :
or
.B ,
in a word that starts with
.BR \- ,
as in
.BR \-Wl,\-rpath,/usr/lib .
.IP \(bu 2
A leading
.B //
before a name with no further
.BR / ,
or a leading
.BR /// ,
loses one slash, so
.B //c
becomes
.BR /c .
This is how to pass a switch that starts with
.BR / ,
as in
.BR "cmd //c" .
.B //server/share
is left as it is, apart from backslashes becoming slashes.
.IP \(bu 2
Windows paths, relative paths, URLs, and words such as
.B foo:/bar
or
.B host:80
are left as they are.
.PP
The usual mistake is a switch that starts with
.BR / :
.B /W3
or
.B /nologo
is a rooted path and becomes a path under the install root.
Write
.BR //W3 .
.PP
For example, with the native busybox
.BR echo :
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ /bin/echo /c/Users/me \-\-dir=/c/src \-I/c/inc \-L/d/lib
C:/Users/me \-\-dir=C:/src \-IC:/inc \-LD:/lib
$ /bin/echo /c/a:/d/b //c /dev/null
C:\ea;D:\eb /c nul
$ /bin/echo C:/x ./y foo:/bar \(aqa /c/b\(aq \(aq*/c/x\(aq
C:/x ./y foo:/bar a /c/b */c/x
$ /bin/echo a/b:/c
a\eb;C:\e
.EE
.RE
.PP
Conversion is off for a program whose environment holds
.BR MSYS_NO_PATHCONV ,
with any value, even empty; it must be exported, or given in front of
the command.
.B MSYS2_ARG_CONV_EXCL
holds
.BR ; -separated
prefixes: an argument starting with one of them is passed unchanged,
and an entry
.B *
excludes every argument.
Both names are matched exactly, and both also act from the command's
own prefix assignments:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ MSYS2_ARG_CONV_EXCL=\-\-dir= /bin/echo \-\-dir=/c/src /c/src
\-\-dir=/c/src C:/src
$ MSYS_NO_PATHCONV=1 /bin/echo /c/src
/c/src
.EE
.RE
.PP
Native mode converts no argument.
.PP
Open descriptors above 2 are passed, as binary descriptors, to a
program that uses a C runtime (msvcrt, UCRT, or an older msvcr DLL),
such as busybox, and to a copy of this shell.
They are not passed to an MSYS2 or Cygwin program, a program with no C
runtime, or a batch file, and a coprocess's descriptors are passed to
no program.
.PP
Process substitution expands to
.BI /dev/fd/ n
in both modes, which builtins and copies of this shell open directly.
An argument to a native program that names an open descriptor 3 or
above that way, whole or after its last
.BR = ,
as in
.BR \-\-file=/dev/fd/63 ,
is replaced by the path of a named pipe,
\fB\e\e.\epipe\eunabashed\-\fIpid\fB\-\fIhex\fB.dll\fR,
which the shell connects to the descriptor; this works for any such
descriptor, not only a process substitution.
At most 16 arguments of one command are replaced, and a program that
opens the same path twice splits the data between the two.
An MSYS2 or Cygwin program cannot use such a pipe, so it gets the path
of a temporary file
.BI sh\-fd\- hex
in the directory that
.B TEMP
(or
.BR TMP )
named when the shell started, removed afterwards.
For
.BI <( list )\fR,
.I list
runs to completion and its whole output is written to the file before
the program starts; beyond 1 GiB the shell gives up and passes
.BI /dev/fd/ n
unchanged.
For
.BI >( list )\fR,
what the program wrote goes to
.I list
after the program exits.
The program shows the temporary file's name where Git Bash would show
.BR /dev/fd/63 .
In msys mode a
.BI /dev/fd/ n
argument that is not replaced becomes
.BI /proc/ pid /fd/ n\fR.
.PP
A script's
.B #!\&
line is read from the first 256 bytes of the file and ends at the first
carriage return or newline.
The interpreter is its first word, and the rest of the line, trimmed of
blanks, is one argument.
The interpreters
.IR /bin/sh ,
.IR /bin/bash ,
.IR /usr/bin/sh ,
and
.IR /usr/bin/bash ,
and
.B /usr/bin/env
with the single argument
.B sh
or
.BR bash ,
mean this shell: the script runs in a copy of this
shell's own program, given
.B \-O msyspath
in msys mode or
.B +O msyspath
in native mode so that it keeps the current mode, then the line's
argument (not for the
.B env
forms), the script, and its arguments.
The copy's other traits follow the program's own name, so in w64devkit
a
.B #!/bin/bash
script run from native
.B sh
runs in
.I sh.exe
with native paths,
.BR OSTYPE=win32 ,
and busybox's startup rules, and a
.B #!/bin/sh
script run from
.B bash
does not enter POSIX mode.
.PP
Any other interpreter is looked for at its path, through the mount
table in msys mode, and in native mode on the current drive with the
five extensions tried; failing that, its last component is searched for
in
.BR PATH .
So
.B #!/usr/bin/env python3
and
.B #!/usr/bin/perl
work when
.B env
and
.B perl
are in
.BR PATH ,
though no
.I /usr/bin
exists.
The interpreter then runs by these same rules and may itself be a
script, up to four levels deep.
A missing interpreter gives
.IB script ": " interp ": bad interpreter: No such file or directory"
and status 126, as does a fifth level, with
.B "Too many levels of symbolic links"
instead.
.PP
In native mode a file whose name ends in
.B .sh
always runs in this shell, as above, whatever its
.B #!\&
line names; the line's argument is still passed, so a
.B .sh
file starting
.B "#!/usr/bin/python3 \-u"
runs as shell code with
.BR \-u .
Also in native mode, as in busybox-w32, a command given as a missing
.BI /bin/ x\fR,
.BI /usr/bin/ x\fR,
.BI /sbin/ x\fR,
or
.BI /usr/sbin/ x
runs
.I x
found in
.BR PATH ,
and
.BR sh ,
.BR bash ,
or
.B ash
runs this shell with the arguments as given, so
.B SHELL=/bin/sh
from a
.B configure
script works.
That copy takes its mode from the name typed, as any copy does:
.I /bin/bash
starts an msys shell, and
.I /bin/sh
a native one unless the install has an
.IR etc/fstab .
.PP
A file with no
.B #!\&
line that is not a Windows image, other than a
.B .sh
file in native mode, runs as a shell script, as bash does,
when its first 80 bytes hold no NUL byte: inside the shell, in a
subshell with options, traps, and functions reset and a new
.BR BASHPID .
In native mode this needs a file that native mode counts as executable;
any other fails with
.B "Permission denied"
and status 126 (see Finding commands above).
Otherwise it fails with
.B "Exec format error"
and status 126.
A file that starts with
.B MZ
but is not a Windows image is handed to Windows as it is, and treated
as a file with no
.B #!\&
line if Windows refuses it.
.PP
Subshells in the foreground, command substitutions, process
substitutions, and every shell stage of a foreground pipeline run inside
the shell, on copies of its state.
A background job, a coprocess, and the shell stages of a background
pipeline run alongside the shell, so they run in a separate copy of the
shell started with the shell's whole state: variables, exported or not,
functions, options, traps, and open descriptors.
A background pipeline is one such process, and its stages run inside
it.
In every case
.B $$
stays the shell's process ID;
.B BASHPID
is the copy's real process ID in a separate copy, and a number that is
no process ID inside the shell.
A background command that runs no shell code, such as
.BR "make &" ,
starts the program directly, and
.B $!\&
is the program's process ID.
If the copy cannot be started, the job runs inside the shell instead,
and the shell waits for it before it exits.
.PP
Windows cannot replace a process, so
.B exec
starts the program as a child.
When nothing else runs in the shell and it is not interactive, the
shell passes on the signals it receives, waits, and exits with the
program's exit code; the program dies if the shell is killed.
Otherwise the program runs as an ordinary child, and the shell exits
with its status.
The program's process ID is not
.BR $$ .
.B exec \-a
and
.B exec \-l
set the first argument a program receives, except for a native program
started from msys mode, which always gets its own full path there, and
a busybox stub in native mode, which gets the applet name.
In native mode the slashes in that argument become backslashes.
.PP
On Windows 8 and later each program also runs in a job object of its
own; see Jobs and signals below.
.SS Files
The shell never reads a reparse point.
A symbolic link or a junction counts as a link when it is not
followed:
.B test \-h
and
.B test \-L
are true for it.
Its target is never read, so
.BR "cd \-P" ,
.BR "pwd \-P" ,
and
.B set \-o physical
resolve nothing: they treat the path as text, as a logical
.B cd
does, and a link in the path stays in
.BR PWD .
In msys mode the physical result is converted back to a POSIX path,
which may spell it through another mount.
.PP
Windows has no file modes, so the shell makes them up.
.BR UID ,
.BR EUID ,
and the shell's group are always 1000, and every file is owned by user
and group 1000.
A directory has mode 0755 and a file 0644, without its write bits
when it has the read-only attribute.
Execute bits follow the rules for what is executable (see Finding
commands above).
ACLs are ignored.
As a result, in
.BR test :
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-r
is true for every existing file, even one the user cannot read.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-w
is false only for a file with the read-only attribute, and true for
every directory.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-x
is true for every directory.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-O
and
.B \-G
are true for every existing file, and
.BR \-u ,
.BR \-g ,
and
.B \-k
are always false.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-ef
compares the volume serial number and the file index.
.PP
The hidden and system attributes are ignored.
Globbing and completion list such files, and only a leading
.B .\&
hides a name.
.PP
Files the shell creates are given mode 0666 (0600 for the history
file), so they are never made read-only.
The umask starts at 022, and
.B umask
reports and changes it, but the shell never applies it.
.PP
The shell opens every file with read, write, and delete sharing, so
other processes may read, write, rename, or delete a file the shell
has open, such as the script it is running.
A
.B >
redirection opens an existing file and truncates it in place, so the
file keeps its identity and a hidden file can be overwritten.
A
.B >>
redirection opens the file for appending only, so every write lands at
the end, including writes by programs that inherit the descriptor.
.PP
The shell writes bytes to files and pipes unchanged, with no LF to
CR LF translation, so text it writes has LF line endings in both modes.
For how it reads carriage returns, see The two modes compared above.
A UTF-8 byte order mark is never stripped: not from a script, a
.B \-c
string, a sourced or startup file, or an inputrc.
A script saved with a byte order mark has no
.B #!\&
line, and its first word begins with the bytes EF BB BF:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ printf \(aq\e357\e273\e277echo hi\en\(aq >bom.sh
$ bash bom.sh
bom.sh: line 1: $\(aq\e357\e273\e277echo\(aq: command not found
.EE
.RE
.PP
Paths passed to Windows are made absolute, and long ones are given the
extended-length prefix, so the shell's own file operations
(redirections,
.BR cd ,
tests, and globbing) have no MAX_PATH limit.
Programs it starts have their own limits.
.PP
File names, arguments, the environment, and the working directory are
UTF-16 to Windows and UTF-8 to the shell.
An unpaired surrogate in a name becomes its WTF-8 encoding, and is
converted back unchanged, so every name round-trips.
A byte that is not part of valid UTF-8 becomes the Latin-1 character
of the same value, so
.B $\(aq\exe9\(aq
and
.B $\(aq\exc3\exa9\(aq
name the same file.
.PP
On a console, the shell's input and output are UTF-8 whatever the
console's code page: it writes with WriteConsoleW, reads with
ReadConsoleW or, in the editor, ReadConsoleInputW, and never changes
the code page.
In its own console output a byte that is not valid UTF-8 shows as the
Latin-1 character of the same value, and so does a character split
across two writes.
.SS Line editing
An interactive shell reads lines with its own editor, which follows GNU
readline: emacs and vi modes, the kill ring, undo, numeric arguments,
incremental search, keyboard macros, history expansion, completion,
and programmable completion
.RB ( complete ,
.BR compgen ,
and
.BR compopt ).
.B bind \-l
lists the bindable functions, and
.B bind
changes key bindings and variables.
.B read \-e
uses the editor too.
.PP
The editor reads standard input and draws on standard error.
When the shell's own input is not a console, as with
.B \-i
and input from a pipe or file, it draws on one line scrolled
sideways,
.B COLUMNS
wide (80 when that is unset or not a number above 1).
It does the same in a console window one row high.
It is not used when standard input is a console and standard error is
not, nor by
.B read \-e
on input that is not a console.
.BR \-\-noediting ,
.B set +o emacs
in emacs mode, or
.B set +o vi
in vi mode turns the editor off; the console's own line input then reads the
line, and a Ctrl-Z at the start of a line is end of file.
.PP
The editor reads its init file on first use, from the file named by
.B INPUTRC
when that is not empty, else from
.IR ~/.inputrc ,
else from
.IR /etc/inputrc .
An unreadable file is skipped silently, and an unreadable
.B INPUTRC
has no fallback.
.I /etc
resolves through the mount table in msys mode, and to the root of the
current drive in native mode.
.B re\-read\-init\-file
(C\-x C\-r) reads the init file found at first use again, or
searches again when none was found; a file read by
.B bind \-f
or
.B $include
does not count.
In msys mode a carriage return at the end of each line is ignored;
native mode keeps it, as readline does, so an inputrc with CR LF line
endings misbehaves there.
.PP
The inputrc directives are
.BR $if ,
.BR $else ,
.BR $endif ,
and
.B $include
(nested at most 32 deep).
.B $if
tests
.BI term= name
against
.B TERM
or its part before the first
.BR \- ,
.B mode=emacs
or
.BR mode=vi ,
.B version
with a comparison operator against 8.3, the application name
.BR bash ,
which is true under every name the shell is started by, and a
variable compared with
.BR = ,
.BR == ,
or
.BR != .
Native mode leaves
.B TERM
unset, so
.B term=
tests are false there unless the user sets it.
Every variable that
.B bind \-v
lists can be set.
These are accepted but have no effect:
.BR blink\-matching\-paren ,
.BR byte\-oriented ,
.BR colored\-completion\-prefix ,
.BR colored\-stats ,
.BR enable\-keypad ,
.BR enable\-meta\-key ,
.BR horizontal\-scroll\-mode ,
.BR input\-meta ,
.BR meta\-flag ,
.BR output\-meta ,
.BR revert\-all\-at\-newline ,
and the two active region colours.
.B convert\-meta
only changes how later bindings of 8-bit keys are stored, as
.B force\-meta\-prefix
does: while either is on, each byte above 0x7F in a key sequence is
bound as ESC and the byte with its high bit cleared.
.B bind \-v
reports
.B prefer\-visible\-bell
as on, but the bell is audible until
.B bell\-style
or
.B prefer\-visible\-bell
is set.
.PP
Besides readline's default bindings, the arrow keys, Home, End,
Ctrl-Left and Ctrl-Right, Alt-Left and Alt-Right, and Ctrl-Delete are
bound before the init file is read.
After it, unless the init file bound them or the editor draws on one
line, Delete, Insert, and the other editing keys get their usual
functions, and in emacs mode PageUp
and PageDown search the history for lines that begin with the text
before the cursor.
.PP
File name completion is case sensitive unless
.B completion\-ignore\-case
is set.
Command completion shows names with their
.B .exe
suffix; in msys mode
.B shopt \-s completion_strip_exe
drops it, unless a file without the suffix exists beside it.
There are no users on Windows, so user name completion finds nothing.
.PP
In an interactive shell, history and history expansion are on.
When
.B HISTFILE
is unset it becomes
.I $HOME/.bash_history
in both modes, or
.I $HOME/.sh_history
when POSIX mode is on before the startup files (see Startup files
above).
.B HISTSIZE
defaults to 500 and
.B HISTFILESIZE
to
.BR HISTSIZE .
The history file is read after the startup files, ignoring a carriage
return at the end of each line.
It is written when the shell exits, but not when the shell dies of a
signal, so closing the console window loses the session's history
unless a
.B HUP
trap exits.
A symbolic link as the history file is written through in place.
.PP
When unset,
.B PS1
is
.B \(aq\es\-\ev\e$\ \(aq
and
.B PS2
is
.BR \(aq>\ \(aq .
.B \es
is
.B $0
after its last
.BR / ,
so a native shell started by a path such as
.I C:\ew64devkit\ebin\esh.exe
shows that whole path in its prompt.
Since the effective user ID is always 1000,
.B \e$
always shows
.BR $ ,
even in an elevated shell.
.PP
Bracketed paste is on: pasted text is inserted as it is, with each CR
turned into LF, and becomes the active region.
It needs a console with escape sequences, and is off when
.B TERM
is
.BR dumb ,
which also makes the editor draw on one line scrolled sideways.
.PP
While it reads a line, the editor puts the console in raw mode and
asks it to interpret escape sequences.
When the console refuses, as on Windows XP and 7, a legacy console, or
any console whose mode cannot be changed, the editor draws through
its own emulator with console functions instead.
The choice depends on the console, not on the Windows version.
After each line the editor restores the console's modes, so programs
run with the console as the shell found it.
.PP
The same emulator also interprets the shell's own output to a console
whose output mode lacked escape sequence processing when the shell
first wrote to it: prompts, error messages, and the output of builtins
such as
.B echo
and
.BR printf .
It handles cursor movement, erasing, the 16 colours, bright and
reverse video, and the window title (OSC 0 and 2), and ignores other
sequences.
It never touches the output of other programs: their escape sequences
reach the console unchanged, and only a console with escape sequence
processing on interprets them.
.SS Jobs and signals
Job control
.RB ( "set \-m" )
is on in an interactive shell with a console, and
.BR jobs ,
.BR fg ,
.BR bg ,
.BR wait ,
.BR kill ,
and
.B disown
work.
Windows cannot stop a process, so no job is ever stopped.
Ctrl-Z stops nothing: the editor's emacs mode leaves it unbound, so it
rings the bell, and vi insert mode inserts it.
.B suspend
fails with the message
.BR "cannot suspend: no job control" .
There are no process groups:
.BI "kill %" n
signals the job's one process.
When the shell exits, background programs keep running;
.B huponexit
and
.B disown \-h
are accepted, but nothing sends
.B SIGHUP
at exit.
.PP
Signal names and numbers are Linux's in both modes (for example
.B USR1
is 10 and
.B PWR
is 30), so
.B kill \-l
differs from Git Bash's.
Signals reach the shell from outside only as console events, a console
resize while the editor reads, or
.B kill
from another copy of the shell:
.IP \(bu 2
Ctrl-C is
.BR SIGINT .
.IP \(bu 2
Ctrl-Break is
.BR SIGQUIT .
.IP \(bu 2
Closing the console window is
.BR SIGHUP .
Windows ends the shell a few seconds later, so a
.B HUP
trap must be brief.
.IP \(bu 2
Resizing the console is
.BR SIGWINCH ,
seen only while the editor reads a line.
.PP
Logoff and shutdown events are not handled: the system's default
handling ends the shell, and no trap runs.
While the editor reads a line, a
.B WINCH
or
.B INT
trap acts at once, and a signal with no trap that would end the shell
ends it; other traps wait until the line is read.
.PP
Ctrl-C while the editor reads a line discards the line and sets
.B $?\&
to 130.
As in bash, a Ctrl-C that arrives while the shell waits for a
foreground program acts only if the program died of it.
A Windows program may end any way it likes on Ctrl-C, so a program
counts as killed by
.B SIGINT
when it exits with code 0xC000013A, or with any non-zero code after a
Ctrl-C.
A native program that handles Ctrl-C and exits non-zero therefore ends
the script or loop that ran it.
A program that the shell was about to start, or that is under 100 ms
old and has not yet attached to the console, may miss the event, so
the shell ends it with code 0xC000013A.
Background programs and programs started under
.B trap \(aq\(aq INT
ignore Ctrl-C.
.B fg
passes Ctrl-C and Ctrl-Break on to the job with
.BR kill .
A job that is a copy of the shell takes them as signals, and the program
it waits for, which ignores Ctrl-C, runs on to its end first, where
bash ends it at once.
.PP
.B kill
to a copy of this shell, including a subshell's
.BR BASHPID ,
delivers the signal, and that shell's traps decide; only
.B KILL
ends it at once, with no
.B EXIT
trap, and
.B STOP
does nothing.
.B kill
to any other program terminates it with exit code 0xC000013A for
.BR INT ,
or 0xE0550000 plus 128+\fIN\fR for signal
.IR N ,
except that signal 0 and
.BR CHLD ,
.BR CONT ,
.BR STOP ,
.BR TSTP ,
.BR TTIN ,
.BR TTOU ,
.BR URG ,
and
.B WINCH
only check that the process exists.
.PP
On Windows 8 and later every program the shell starts runs in a job
object of its own.
.BI "kill \-" sig " \-" pid
on one of the last 64 background programs ends it together with
everything it started, unless that program is a copy of this shell,
which takes the signal as above, or the signal is one that only checks
that the process exists.
Before Windows 8, and for any other process,
.BI \- pid
means the process alone.
.PP
The shell decodes a program's exit code as death by a signal where
one fits, and reports it with the signal's name, as in bash:
.TP
\fB0xE0550000\fR + 128+\fIN\fR
Signal
.IR N ,
from
.B kill
or a shell that died of it.
.TP
.BR 0xC0000005 ", " 0xC00000FD
Access violation and stack overflow:
.B SIGSEGV
(status 139).
.TP
.B 0xC000001D
Illegal instruction:
.B SIGILL
(132).
.TP
.B 0xC0000094
Integer division by zero:
.B SIGFPE
(136).
.TP
.B 0xC0000409
Fast fail:
.B SIGABRT
(134).
.TP
.B 0xC000013A
Ctrl-C:
.B SIGINT
(130).
.TP
.B 0xC0000135
A missing DLL: status 127, with a message that names the DLL.
.PP
Any other exit code gives its low byte.
The C runtime's
.BR abort ()
exits with code 3, which gives status 3.
.PP
The shell emulates
.BR SIGPIPE :
a shell process or subshell that writes to a pipe with no reader ends
with status 141, unless
.B PIPE
is trapped or ignored; then the write fails with
.BR "Broken pipe" .
Programs get no emulation and behave as their runtime does.
.PP
Recursion that exhausts the stack kills the shell or subshell by
.BR SIGSEGV :
no
.B EXIT
trap runs, the status is 139, and the parent reports
.BR "Segmentation fault" .
A shell that dies of signal
.I N
in any way exits with code 0xE0550000 plus 128+\fIN\fR, or 0xC000013A
for
.BR SIGINT .
Another copy of the shell sees status 128+\fIN\fR either way, and so
does a Windows parent that keeps only the low byte, except for
.BR SIGINT ,
whose low byte is 58.
Git Bash reports 0xC000013A as status 130 and the other codes as
status 127.
For example, a shell killed by
.B SIGTERM
exits with code 0xE055008F.
.SS "Differences from bash"
Apart from paths, devices, and the running of programs, described above,
unabashed differs from bash 5.3 in these ways, in both modes unless one
is named.
.IP \(bu 2
Only the C and POSIX locales and UTF-8 locales exist.
Characters are UTF-8 when the first non-empty one of
.BR LC_ALL ,
.BR LC_CTYPE ,
and
.B LANG
names a UTF-8 locale, and single bytes otherwise.
Every UTF-8 locale counts as installed, and messages are never translated.
On Windows any other locale name acts as C without a warning.
In msys mode a built-in table of languages gives the decimal point;
native mode always uses a period.
.IP \(bu 2
CPU time is not tracked:
.B times
and the
.B time
keyword report zero user and system time.
.B time
reports real time correctly.
This is an open issue.
.IP \(bu 2
.B BASHPID
in a subshell, command substitution, or pipeline stage that runs inside
the shell is a virtual ID, not a process ID, which
.B kill
accepts.
In a background job's child shell it is that process's real ID.
.IP \(bu 2
.B RANDOM
and
.B SRANDOM
come from PCG64.
Assigning a seed to
.B RANDOM
gives a different sequence than bash, but the same one on every platform.
Every subshell, command substitution, pipeline stage, and child reseeds
on first use.
.B SRANDOM
cannot be seeded: assigning to it makes it an ordinary variable with
that value, where bash ignores the assignment.
.IP \(bu 2
The emulated generators, such as
.IR /dev/zero ,
are pipes when read, so
.B test \-p
is true on a descriptor open on one (see Devices above).
.IP \(bu 2
.I /dev/clipboard
holds only the clipboard's Unicode text, where Git Bash also keeps
Cygwin's own copy of the bytes, so text written there ends at a NUL
byte and invalid UTF-8 does not survive the round trip.
It holds at most 64 MiB, or 2 MiB in the 32-bit build, past which writes
fail with
.BR "Cannot allocate memory" .
Everything written through one open adds to the same text, a child's
output included, where a Git Bash child starts its own text, lost at
the parent's next write.
As in Git Bash, each of the shell's writes is a separate change to the
clipboard.
A clipboard held open by another program is an error, where Git Bash
reads nothing and drops the write.
.IP \(bu 2
Process substitution shows
.BI /dev/fd/ N
inside the shell, but a native program gets the path of a named pipe
under
.IR \e\e.\epipe\e ,
and an MSYS2 program the path of a temporary file (see Running programs
above).
.IP \(bu 2
There is no
.I /dev/tcp
or
.IR /dev/udp .
.IP \(bu 2
There is no mail checking.
.BR MAIL ,
.BR MAILPATH ,
and
.B MAILCHECK
are ignored and not set, and
.B shopt \-s mailwarn
has no effect.
.IP \(bu 2
.B TMOUT
is only the default timeout for
.BR read :
an interactive shell does not exit when no line arrives within that
many seconds.
.IP \(bu 2
Native mode never enters POSIX mode because the shell is named
.BR sh ;
only the options and variables given under Invocation above, or
.BR "set \-o posix" ,
do.
.IP \(bu 2
The umask is the shell's own value, 0022 at startup.
It is never read from or applied to the system, so
.B umask
changes what
.B umask
prints and nothing else.
.IP \(bu 2
.B GROUPS
has one element, the shell's group ID, which is 1000 on Windows, as are
.B UID
and
.BR EUID .
.IP \(bu 2
On Windows
.B HOSTNAME
is the NetBIOS computer name, not the DNS host name.
.IP \(bu 2
Nesting deep enough to exhaust the parser's stack, such as thousands of
nested subshells written
.BR "( ( ( " ...,
makes the shell exit with status 139 and no message, where bash reports
a syntax error.
This is an open issue.
.IP \(bu 2
A trap action that sends its own signal stops after ten nested levels,
and the shell exits with status 139.
.IP \(bu 2
.BI ~ user
is left as typed, there being no user database, and so is
.B ~
when
.B HOME
is unset.
.IP \(bu 2
.B jobs \-l
shows a background pipeline as one line with one process ID, that of
the child shell running it, which is also
.BR $! .
.IP \(bu 2
The end of a signalled job is seen at once, so
.B kill %1; wait %1
can give 127 where bash gives 143.
.IP \(bu 2
When every stage that runs inside the shell is blocked on another, the
shell prints
.B "unabashed: deadlock: every task is blocked"
and fails the blocked reads and writes, where bash would hang.
.IP \(bu 2
A Windows pipe can take far more than 64 KiB before its reader exits, so
the writer in
.BI "f | " reader
whose reader quits early may finish with status 0 instead of 141.
.IP \(bu 2
.B exec
on Windows starts the program as a child and waits for it, so its
process ID is not
.BR $$ .
.IP \(bu 2
A background job that cannot get a process of its own runs inside the
shell, which waits for it before exiting.
.IP \(bu 2
Traps for
.BR TERM ,
.BR HUP ,
.BR QUIT ,
.BR ALRM ,
and the stop signals that arrive while a line is being edited run when
the line is done, where bash runs them at once.
.IP \(bu 2
.B enable \-f
fails with
.BR "dynamic loading not available" ,
although
.B BASH_LOADABLES_PATH
has its Linux default.
.IP \(bu 2
.B ulimit
on Windows shows a fixed table like Git Bash's, in which
.B \-s
is 16384, the shell's own stack.
Only
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-n ,
.BR \-s ,
and
.B \-v
can be changed.
No value reaches the system or a program, and only
.B \-n
has an effect: it bounds the descriptors that
.BI { var }
redirections take.
.IP \(bu 2
.B BASH_VERSION
is
.B 5.3.0(1)\-release
in every build, and a 32-bit build reports
.B i686
in
.B MACHTYPE
and
.BR HOSTTYPE .
.IP \(bu 2
The text of
.B help
and
.B \-\-help
is unabashed's own, and mentions Windows only where msys mode's
.B help pwd
describes
.BR \-W .
There is no
.B help cygpath
topic; use
.BR "cygpath \-\-help" .
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-\-debugger
looks only for
.IR /usr/local/share/bashdb/bashdb\-main.inc .
.SH OPTIONS
Long options come first, each spelled with two dashes or one, as in
.B \-\-login
or
.BR \-login .
The first argument that is not a long option ends them.
After a single-letter option,
.B \-\-login
is rejected with
\fIname\fB: \-\-: invalid option\fR,
and
.B \-login
is taken as the letters
.BR \-l ,
.BR \-o ,
and so on.
Single-letter options follow, grouped or apart, with
.B \-
to turn one on and
.B +
to turn it off; for
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-s ,
.BR \-l ,
and
.B \-D
the sign makes no difference.
.B \-o
and
.B \-O
each take the next argument as an option name.
An argument of
.B \-
or
.B \-\-
ends the options.
An unknown option prints
.IB name ": " option ": invalid option"
and the usage text on standard error, and the shell exits with status
2.
The options and their order follow
.BR "sh \-\-help" .
.TP
.B \-\-debug
Accepted, and has no effect.
.TP
.B \-\-debugger
Source
.I /usr/local/share/bashdb/bashdb\-main.inc
with
.B extdebug
on, before running a script or in a non-interactive shell.
That file is not part of w64devkit, so the shell reports it missing,
warns that debugging mode is disabled, and carries on.
.TP
.B \-\-dump\-po\-strings
Like
.BR \-D ,
but print each string as a gettext PO entry: a
.BI "#: " file : line
comment, then
.B msgid
and an empty
.BR msgstr .
.TP
.B \-\-dump\-strings
The same as
.BR \-D .
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print the version line, the usage text, and two lines pointing to
.B help
on standard output, then exit with status 0, without looking at the
single-letter options.
The usage text names the shell by its zeroth argument, often a full path,
converted in msys mode as for
.B $0
(see Invocation above).
.TP
.BI "\-\-init\-file " file
The same as
.BR \-\-rcfile .
.TP
.B \-\-login
The same as
.BR \-l .
.TP
.B \-\-noediting
Do not turn on
.B emacs
editing in an interactive shell, so it reads lines without the line
editor unless
.B \-o vi
or
.BR "\-o emacs" ,
on the command line or in a startup file, turns it on.
.TP
.B \-\-noprofile
Read no login files.
.TP
.B \-\-norc
In msys mode, read neither
.I /etc/bash.bashrc
nor
.IR ~/.bashrc .
It does nothing in native mode, where
.B ENV
is still read.
.TP
.B \-\-posix
Enter POSIX mode before the startup files, setting
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
to
.BR y .
.TP
.B \-\-pretty\-print
Print each command of the script or standard input in canonical form
instead of running it.
It is ignored with
.BR \-c ,
and ignored with a warning in an interactive shell.
.TP
.BI "\-\-rcfile " file
In msys mode, read
.I file
in place of
.I ~/.bashrc
in an interactive shell that is not a login shell, after
.IR /etc/bash.bashrc ,
unless the shell is named
.BR sh ,
is in POSIX mode, or has
.BR \-\-norc .
It is ignored in native mode.
Without
.IR file ,
the shell prints
.IB name ": rcfile: option requires an argument"
(or
.B init\-file
for
.BR \-\-init\-file )
and exits with status 2.
.TP
.B \-\-restricted
The same as
.BR \-r .
.TP
.B \-\-verbose
The same as
.BR \-v .
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print
.BI "unabashed, version 5.3.0(1)\-release (" MACHTYPE ),
the bash release that scripts look for, then
.BR "unabashed 0.1.1" ,
the release of unabashed itself,
and a line saying that the software is in the public domain, then exit
with status 0, without looking at the single-letter options.
.B \-\-help
takes precedence.
.TP
.B \-i
Be interactive.
.B +i
cancels an earlier
.BR \-i ,
but a shell that reads commands from a console, as described under
Invocation above, is interactive regardless: one with no
.BR \-c ,
no
.I file
(or with
.BR \-s ),
and standard input and standard error both consoles.
.TP
.B \-l
Be a login shell.
.TP
.B \-r
Be restricted.
.B +r
is an invalid option.
.TP
.B \-s
Read commands from standard input even when arguments remain, which
become the positional parameters.
It has no effect with
.BR \-c .
.TP
.B \-D
Print each
.B $\(dq...\(dq\&
string in the script, the
.B \-c
command, or standard input, and run nothing.
.TP
.B \-c
Run the first argument after the options as a command.
Without one, the shell prints
.IB name ": \-c: option requires an argument"
and exits with status 2.
.TP
.BI "\-O " option
Turn on the
.B shopt
option
.IR option ,
or turn it off with
.BR +O ,
after the interactive defaults and before the startup files.
.B \-O
as the last argument prints every
.B shopt
option and its state, and
.B +O
prints them as
.B shopt
commands; the shell then carries on.
An unknown name prints an error and the shell exits with status 2.
The hidden
.B msyspath
option is accepted here.
.TP
.B \-abefhkmnptuvxBCEHPT
Turn on the
.B set
option of that letter, or turn it off with
.BR + ,
as
.B set
does
.RB ( "help set" ).
.B \-h
is
.BR hashall ,
not help.
.TP
.BI "\-o " option
Turn on the
.B set
option named
.IR option ,
or turn it off with
.BR +o .
.B \-o
as the last argument prints every
.B set
option and its state, and
.B +o
prints them as
.B set
commands; the shell then carries on.
An unknown name prints an error and the shell exits with status 2.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Only the variables that unabashed treats in its own way, or differently
by mode, are listed.
See Environment above for how the environment is imported and passed
on.
.TP
.B BASH_ENV
A file a non-interactive shell reads at startup, in msys mode only, and
not when it is named
.B sh
or is in POSIX mode.
Native mode never reads it.
.TP
.B BB_OVERRIDE_APPLETS
In native mode, the applets whose busybox alias stubs run as ordinary
programs instead of as
.I busybox.exe
directly.
Names are separated by spaces, commas, or semicolons, may start with
.BR + ,
and match ignoring case;
.B \-
names every applet.
It must be exported.
.TP
.B COMSPEC
The command interpreter that runs
.I .bat
and
.I .cmd
files, from the program's environment.
It defaults to
.I System32\ecmd.exe
in the Windows directory.
.TP
.B ENV
A file an interactive shell reads at startup: always in native mode, and
in msys mode only when the shell is named
.B sh
or is in POSIX mode.
Native mode expands no
.B ~
in it.
.TP
.B HISTFILE
The history file, in both modes by default
.IR ~/.bash_history ,
or
.I ~/.sh_history
when POSIX mode comes from
.BR \-\-posix ,
.BR "\-o posix" ,
an inherited
.BR SHELLOPTS ,
or
.BR POSIXLY_CORRECT ;
a shell named
.B sh
keeps
.IR ~/.bash_history .
.TP
.B HOME
When unset at startup, the shell sets it from
.BR USERPROFILE :
in POSIX form and exported in msys mode, with forward slashes and not
exported in native mode.
Msys mode imports an inherited value in POSIX form and gives native
programs the Windows form.
.TP
.B INPUTRC
The line editor's settings file, by default
.I ~/.inputrc
and then
.IR /etc/inputrc ,
which in msys mode goes through the mount table and in native mode is on
the current drive.
.TP
.BR LANG ", " LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE
Whether the shell handles UTF-8 characters, as described in Differences
from bash above.
.TP
.B LD_LIBRARY_PATH
In msys mode, imported as a
.BR : -separated
list and given to native programs as a
.BR ; -separated
list of Windows paths.
.TP
.B MACHTYPE
Set to
.IB arch \-pc\-cygwin
in msys mode and
.IB arch \-w64\-mingw32
in native mode, where
.I arch
is
.B x86_64
or
.BR i686 .
.TP
.B MSYS2_ARG_CONV_EXCL
In msys mode, argument prefixes, separated by
.BR ; ,
that are not converted for native programs;
.B *
excludes every argument.
It is read from the program's environment, so it must be exported or
given in front of the command.
.TP
.B MSYS2_ENV_CONV_EXCL
In msys mode, prefixes of
.IB name = value
entries, separated by
.BR ; ,
that are not converted for native programs;
.B *
excludes all of them.
The path variables listed under
.B MSYS_NO_PATHCONV
are converted regardless.
.TP
.B MSYS_NO_PATHCONV
In msys mode, when present in a native program's environment, even
empty, no argument and no environment value is converted, except
.BR PATH ,
.BR ORIGINAL_PATH ,
.BR LD_LIBRARY_PATH ,
.BR HOME ,
.BR SHELL ,
.BR TMPDIR ,
.BR TMP ,
and
.BR TEMP .
.TP
.B ORIGINAL_PATH
In msys mode, converted as
.B PATH
is.
.TP
.B OSTYPE
Set to
.B cygwin
in msys mode and
.B win32
in native mode.
.TP
.B PATH
Where commands are found.
In msys mode it is imported as a
.BR : -separated
list of POSIX paths, becomes
.I /usr/bin
when unset, and reaches native programs as a
.BR ; -separated
list of Windows paths.
In native mode it is
.BR ; -separated,
an assignment turns
.B :
into
.B ;
except after a drive letter, it becomes the shell's own directory, not
exported, when unset, and programs get it unchanged.
See Finding commands above.
.TP
.B PWD
Always set and exported.
Msys mode keeps an inherited value that names the working directory;
native mode never inherits it.
.TP
.B SHELL
When unset, set to
.I /usr/bin/bash
in msys mode and to the shell's own path in native mode, without being
exported.
Msys mode converts it as it does
.BR HOME .
.TP
.BR SYSTEMROOT ", " WINDIR
The Windows directory, for
.B cygpath \-W
and
.BR \-S ,
with
.I C:/Windows
as the last resort.
Msys mode adds both to a native program's environment when they are
missing.
.TP
.BR TEMP ", " TMP
In msys mode without an
.IR etc/fstab ,
.I /tmp
is the directory in
.BR TEMP ,
else in
.BR TMP ,
as set at startup.
Msys mode converts both as it does
.BR HOME .
Here-documents that need a file get one in the directory in
.BR TMP ,
else in
.BR TEMP ,
as the shell inherited them; assigning either in the shell changes
neither.
.TP
.B TERM
The terminal type.
When it is unset, msys mode sets and exports
.BR xterm\-256color ,
and native mode leaves it unset; msys mode also drops an inherited
.BR TERM=msys .
On Windows the line editor draws on one line, as on a dumb terminal,
when
.B TERM
is
.BR dumb ,
and also in the other cases given under Line editing above.
.TP
.B TMPDIR
Where
.B fc
and
.B edit\-and\-execute\-command
put their temporary file, when it names a directory; otherwise
.IR /tmp .
In native mode that is
.I /tmp
on the root of the current drive, which usually does not exist, so
there
.B fc
fails with
.B "cannot open temp file"
unless
.B TMPDIR
is set.
Msys mode converts it as it does
.BR HOME .
.TP
.B TZ
The time zone, when exported; otherwise the Windows zone.
Msys mode reads zoneinfo files, by absolute path or under
.IR /usr/share/zoneinfo ,
and native mode reads none.
Msys mode passes
.B TZ
to a native program only when it is empty, or does not start with
.B :
and holds only letters, digits,
.BR + ,
.BR \- ,
and
.BR : .
.TP
.B USERNAME
In native mode, the default for
.B USER
and
.BR LOGNAME ,
which are not exported.
.TP
.B USERPROFILE
The default for
.BR HOME .
.SH EXIT STATUS
The shell exits with the status of the last command it ran, or with one
of these.
.TP
.B 1
A builtin failed, such as
.B cd
to a missing directory.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error, a syntax error, an invalid startup option, or an
exhausted memory reservation, which prints
.BR "bash: out of memory" .
.TP
.B 126
A command, or the script named on the command line, was found but cannot
be run.
.TP
.B 127
A command, or the script named on the command line, was not found.
.TP
.BI 128+ n
The shell, a subshell, or the last command was killed by signal
.IR n ,
for example 130 by SIGINT (Ctrl-C), 141 by SIGPIPE, and 139 by SIGSEGV
when shell recursion exhausts the stack, which runs no EXIT trap.
Parser overflow and runaway trap recursion also exit with 139, but
without a signal.
.PP
A native program's crash code counts as a signal, as in Cygwin:
0xC0000005 and 0xC00000FD as SIGSEGV, 0xC000001D as SIGILL, 0xC0000094
as SIGFPE, 0xC000013A as SIGINT, and 0xC0000409 as SIGABRT.
0xC0000135, a missing DLL, gives 127 and a message naming the DLL.
Any other exit code gives its low byte.
.PP
When the shell itself dies by a signal on Windows, its parent sees exit
code 0xC000013A for SIGINT, as for a native program ended by Ctrl-C, and
0xE0550000 plus 128+\fIn\fR for any other signal
.IR n ,
such as 0xE055008B for SIGSEGV.
The low byte is the status for a parent that keeps only the low byte,
such as busybox
.BR sh ;
a Cygwin or MSYS2 shell, such as Git Bash, reports 127 for the 0xE055
codes.
Another unabashed takes either code for death by the signal.
.SH EXAMPLES
The examples run at w64devkit's prompt, a native-mode
.BR sh .
.PP
Convert paths with the msys-mode
.BR cygpath :
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ bash \-c \(aqcygpath \-w /c/Windows/System32\(aq
C:\eWindows\eSystem32
$ bash \-c \(aqcygpath \-u "C:\eProgram Files"\(aq
/c/Program Files
.EE
.RE
.PP
A
.B #!/bin/bash
script run from native mode stays in native mode, so run a script
written for Git Bash with
.BR bash :
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ cat hello.sh
#!/bin/bash
echo $OSTYPE
$ ./hello.sh
win32
$ bash hello.sh
cygwin
.EE
.RE
.PP
The kit's
.I etc/profile
exports
.B PATH_SEPARATOR=\(aq;\(aq
for native mode, but a login
.B bash
reads it too, and one started from native mode inherits it, although
msys mode splits
.B PATH
on colons, so unset it before running a
.B configure
script with
.BR bash .
.PP
Msys mode converts arguments that look like POSIX paths for a native
program:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ bash \-c \(aqbusybox echo /c/src \-I/c/inc\(aq
C:/src \-IC:/inc
.EE
.RE
.PP
Pass every argument unconverted:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ bash \-c \(aqMSYS_NO_PATHCONV=1 busybox echo /c/src \-I/c/inc\(aq
/c/src \-I/c/inc
.EE
.RE
.PP
Leave only the arguments that start with
.B \-I
unconverted:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ bash \-c \(aqMSYS2_ARG_CONV_EXCL=\-I busybox echo /c/src \-I/c/inc\(aq
C:/src \-I/c/inc
.EE
.RE
.PP
In msys mode, give a device to a native program by redirection, since
its name is converted:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ bash \-c \(aqod \-An \-tx1 \-N8 /dev/zero\(aq
od: /Device/Null: No such file or directory
$ bash \-c \(aqod \-An \-tx1 \-N8 </dev/zero\(aq
 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00
.EE
.RE
.PP
Switch a native shell to msys mode:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ cd C:/Windows
$ pwd
C:/Windows
$ shopt \-s msyspath
$ pwd
/c/Windows
.EE
.RE
.PP
Start a login shell that reads no profiles, with the long option first:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ sh \-\-noprofile \-l
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
The Windows builds import only
.IR kernel32.dll ,
and look up anything newer than Windows XP at run time, with a fallback.
The i686 build runs on Windows XP and is large-address-aware, so it can
use 4 GiB on 64-bit Windows.
The x86_64 build is untested on Windows XP x64.
.PP
On Windows the shell runs on a 16 MiB stack of its own, whatever the
program's header says, and
.B ulimit \-s
reports 16384.
Subshells, command substitutions, and pipeline stages that run inside
the shell get 8 MiB, so recursion there fails sooner.
Recursion that exhausts a stack kills the shell or subshell by SIGSEGV.
.PP
Memory is reserved as address space and committed as it is used: 16 GiB
for each arena on 64-bit builds and 256 MiB on 32-bit builds.
On 32-bit builds the first level of command substitution reserves 128
MiB, and a subshell inside the shell or a deeper command substitution 16
MiB.
Running out prints
.B bash: out of memory
and ends the whole process with status 2, as does nesting command
substitutions, traps, and prompts 64 levels deep.
.PP
Console input and output are UTF-8 to the shell whatever the console's
code page, while data through files and pipes passes unchanged (see
Files above).
.PP
busybox's own shell is still available as
.BR "busybox sh" .
.PP
On Linux and macOS unabashed builds as a plain bash 5.3 clone, linked
against libc only.
It has no path modes, no mount table, no
.BR cygpath ,
and none of the Windows behaviour above: paths pass through unchanged,
carriage returns are kept, and
.I /dev
and
.I /proc
are the system's.
It has real processes, process groups, job control with stopped jobs,
user IDs, and resource limits.
Its
.B MACHTYPE
and
.B OSTYPE
always name x86_64 Linux.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR make (1),
.BR w64devkit (1)
